<section id="A6.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.7 </span>Robustness to Random Seed</h3>

<section id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Protocol.</h5>

<div id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All numbers reported in the main paper (Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ HyperEyes: Dual-Grained Efficiency-Aware Reinforcement Learning for Parallel Multimodal Search Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)
are obtained with a fixed random seed (<span id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">seed=42</span>) for both
training and rollout sampling. To assess the stability of our
post-training recipe, we additionally re-run the entire reinforcement
learning stage of the smaller HyperEyes-30B (RL) variant
under <math id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=3</annotation></semantics></math> independent random seeds (seeds <math id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\{42,\ 1234,\ 2026\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>42</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 1234</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 2026</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\{42,\ 1234,\ 2026\}</annotation></semantics></math>), keeping the SFT checkpoint,
training data, hyperparameters, and infrastructure identical across
runs. For each seed, we evaluate the final RL checkpoint on all six
benchmarks using the same protocol described in
Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ HyperEyes: Dual-Grained Efficiency-Aware Reinforcement Learning for Parallel Multimodal Search Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, and report the mean <math id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> standard
deviation of accuracy (%) and average tool turns. We focus on the
30B variant due to compute constraints; the 235B variant is reported
with a single seed throughout.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Aggregate results.</h5>

<div id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A6.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Aggregate results. ‣ F.7 Robustness to Random Seed ‣ Appendix F Training Details ‣ HyperEyes: Dual-Grained Efficiency-Aware Reinforcement Learning for Parallel Multimodal Search Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> summarizes the per-benchmark mean <math id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>
std across the three seeds, alongside the single-seed numbers
reported in the main paper. The standard deviation of accuracy is at
most <math id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{1.22}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{1.22}</annotation></semantics></math> points across all six benchmarks (achieved
on MMSearch) and only <math id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{0.19}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.19</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{0.19}</annotation></semantics></math> on the six-benchmark
average; the standard deviation of the average tool turns is at most
<math id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{0.10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{0.10}</annotation></semantics></math> per-benchmark and only <math id="A6.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{0.02}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.02</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{0.02}</annotation></semantics></math> on
average. Both the accuracy and the efficiency claims of HyperEyes are
therefore stable under different random initializations of the RL
stage.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.T11" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A6.T11.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 11</span>: </span><span id="A6.T11.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Robustness of HyperEyes-30B (RL) to the RL random seed.
Mean <math id="A6.T11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> std across <math id="A6.T11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=3</annotation></semantics></math> independent RL runs from the same SFT
checkpoint (seeds <math id="A6.T11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\{42,1234,2026\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>42</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1234</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2026</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\{42,1234,2026\}</annotation></semantics></math>). “Reported” denotes the
single-seed number in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ HyperEyes: Dual-Grained Efficiency-Aware Reinforcement Learning for Parallel Multimodal Search Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
(<span id="A6.T11.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">seed=42</span>). <em id="A6.T11.7.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Acc</em> is benchmark accuracy (%);
<em id="A6.T11.7.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Turns</em> is the average number of tool calls per query.</span></figcaption>
<table id="A6.T11.8" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A6.T11.8.1" class="ltx_tr">
